  "body": "The DXF import and Wire Contour selection prerequisites merged in #3193 and #3205. This PR adds the native zero-step inspection export.\n\n## Change\n\nUse `camcraft-cli export-agie-inspection <project> <operation> <new-directory>` for a saved operation.\n\nA user can create an unassigned wire inspection operation on the Post page and choose one placed, closed DXF chain and an internal start. The user can save the operation and export a native Classic Cut 2S ISO/SBR/SBL directory with zero working steps. The CLI and MCP use the same entry points.\n\nThe exporter preserves the nominal line and circular arc geometry. It checks the source selection, closure, winding, rounding, arc extrema, start disk and supported mounted machine axes. It refuses a changed or unsuitable selection and a destination that exists. An inspection work does not become a qualified machining operation.\n\nThis first setup uses 10 mm height, a 3 mm internal start and a 0.25 mm wire. The reference piece has unknown physical X/Y dimensions. Its controller fields store zero length and breadth. The receipt identifies the unverified fixture, erosion settings, physical Z and perpendicular controller lead. It describes the controller’s quality request separately from an assigned erosion process. The nominal setup does not promise a historical start seam, lead or cut sequence.\n\n## Verification on the refreshed head\n\nCommit `d982d452b` merges upstream main at `4e0c81964` into the contributor branch. The only manual resolution was the generated MCP reference. The compiled registry and the published reference list the same 119 tools. A trial merge with main at `6308a5db5` completed without conflicts.\n\nThe targeted author check passed 104 tests on this head. These covered post (6), MCP (3), desktop (3), application regressions (74) and geometry (18). Strict all-target Clippy passed for the four touched crates. The complete quick selector listed 1,048 steps. The author ran the targeted quick alternative.\n\nThe refreshed CLI imported an unchanged drawing, reopened the saved operations and exported two native jobs. All six files matched the controller-checked files byte for byte. Each receipt reported zero working steps. Repeat exports refused to overwrite either job.\n\nThe official heavy gate passed all 254 selected steps in 62.7 minutes on this revision. The command exited with status 0.\n\n```sh\ncargo xtask gate heavy --base 4e0c81964\n```\n\n## Earlier verification on `d13c5eed1`\n\nContributor head: `d13c5eed15251af0a903396c1d96bba298b644d0`. Parent: `2d0fa64841a1b42dbacb330f75f6bab61897da10`.\n\nAll eight scoped tests passed on this earlier head: two post tests, three MCP tests and three desktop tests. Strict all-target Clippy passed for geometry, post, app and UI. The independent synthetic export preserved every directed edge, arc center and resource link. It wrote one start, one work and zero steps. The checks include:\n\n- `agie_inspection_accepts_listed_chain_and_checks_mounted_travel_in_the_wcs`\n- `saved_selected_opening_exports_zero_steps_through_the_desktop_action`\n- `unassigned_inspection_opening_survives_undo_redo_and_project_reopen`\n- `setup_post_inspection_fields_and_submit_fit_the_visible_panel`\n\nOn this earlier revision, 252 of 253 heavy steps passed. The bulk release build failed to link two test binaries when the device ran out of space. The refreshed revision has its own gate run.\n\nThe desktop authoring and selection use a real undoable operation. The documented MCP reference was regenerated from the compiled CLI. The public selection returned by `list_features` can be passed directly to `create_agie_inspection`. A regression narrows the mount travel and reverses an axis. Each case refuses before file creation.\n\nWe used an offline VM running a copy of the actual Agie Classic Cut 2S control. The control opened native files produced by CAMCraft. We checked the program and ISO identities, nominal orientation, hole size and position, setup height, zero separation and zero working steps. Each work-level formal check blocked on undefined working technology. Each whole-piece check also flagged the low zero X/Y dimensions. Both control previews rendered the bound nominal profiles. An identical repeat import with the quality already present kept zero assigned working steps. Repeating an export to the same directory preserved the old files. No physical cut was made.\n\nNo model data, original job or simulator software is included. The public geometry tests are original synthetic cases. This inspection slice does not qualify a working erosion process, a physical slug restraint or an automatic cutting release. #3178 remains open for qualified curved die cutting. #3179 retains its physical process requirements.\n\n## Desktop comparison\n\nBoth images use the same synthetic project and 750 by 830 pixel setup panel. The parent has the existing post controls. The new form shows the selected opening, wire and both start coordinates.\n\n| Parent | This change |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![Parent setup Post panel](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qsmits/Camcraft/69865cfd9c4856f159fd63c5ba79b7b08724e264/issue-3179/setup-before.png) | ![Setup Post panel with the Agie inspection form](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qsmits/Camcraft/69865cfd9c4856f159fd63c5ba79b7b08724e264/issue-3179/setup-after.png) |\n\nEffort: high. No escalation.\n\nChangelog: Export a selected closed DXF opening as a native, zero-step Classic Cut 2S inspection setup from desktop, CLI or MCP.\n\nRefs #3178\n\nRefs #3179\n",
